{"name":"Natural Order","hex":"#77b033","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"natural","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Existing in nature.\nExisting in the nature of a person or thing; innate, not acquired or learned. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Existing in the nature of a person or thing; innate, not acquired or learned. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native inhabitant of a place, country, etc.","A note that is not or is no longer to be modified by an accidental."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/natural"},{"term":"order","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Arrangement, disposition, or sequence.","A position in an arrangement, disposition, or sequence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set in some sort of order.","To arrange, set in proper order."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/order"}],"entries":[{"title":"Natural order (philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"In philosophy, the natural order is the moral source from which natural law seeks to derive its authority. Natural order encompasses the natural relations of beings to one another in the absence of law, which natural law attempts to reinforce. In contrast, divine law seeks authority from God, and positive law seeks authority from government."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg/330px-Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_order_(philosophy)"},{"title":"Input hypothesis","blocks":[{"text":"The input hypothesis, also known as the monitor model, is a group of five hypotheses of second-language acquisition developed by the linguist Stephen Krashen in the 1970s and 1980s. Krashen originally formulated the input hypothesis as just one of the five hypotheses, but over time the term has come to refer to the five hypotheses as a group. The hypotheses are the input hypothesis, the acquisition–learning hypothesis, the monitor hypothesis, the natural order hypothesis and the affective filter hypothesis. The input hypothesis was first published in 1977."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Comprehensible_input_hypothesis.png/330px-Comprehensible_input_hypothesis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Input_hypothesis"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg/500px-The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg/1280px-The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg/500px-The_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Blue Marble, which is a famous view of the Earth, taken in 1972 by the crew of Apollo 17","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Blue_Marble%2C_AS17-148-22727.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Tectonic_plate_boundaries.png/500px-Tectonic_plate_boundaries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Tectonic_plate_boundaries.png/500px-Tectonic_plate_boundaries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three types of geological plate tectonic boundaries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATectonic_plate_boundaries.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Pangea_animation_03.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Pangea_animation_03.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An animation showing the movement of the continents from the separation of Pangaea until the present day","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APangea_animation_03.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Hyperia.jpg/500px-Hyperia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Hyperia.jpg/1280px-Hyperia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Hyperia.jpg/500px-Hyperia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plankton inhabit oceans, seas and lakes, and have existed in various forms for at least 2 billion years.[25]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHyperia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/500px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.